248U  c                                                                         
248U  c  Other than the updated theoretical references, calculated Q values     
248U 2c  and separation energies, there have been no changes in this dataset    
248U 3c  from the previous evaluation (ENSDF online version A.A. Sonzogni and   
248U 4c  B. Singh)                                                              
248U  c  1960Di03: {+248}U measured indirectly through the detection of         
248U 2c  {+248}Cm (T{-1/2}=3.48|*10{+5} y) in the debris of the first           
248U 3c  large-scale thermonuclear test (Ivy Mike) of November 1, 1952 in the   
248U 4c  Pacific Ocean. Airborne and condensed samples were collected,          
248U 5c  followed by chemical extraction and purification. The isotopic         
248U 6c  composition of Pu, Am and Cm fractions were determined by using mass   
248U 7c  spectrometers, while the abundances of all the transcurium elements    
248U 8c  were measured by the detection of radiations, primarily |a particles.  
248U 9c  Mass abundance of A=239-255 uranium isotopes at zero time were         
248U ac  deduced, which varied from 1.0 for {+239}U to 5.7|*10{+-11} for        
248U bc  {+255}U. The heavy uranium isotopes are expected to be produced in an  
248U cc  environment of unusually high neutron flux (time-integrated flux of    
248U dc  |?10{+24} n/cm{+2}) through successive neutron captures in {+238}U,    
248U ec  with neutron energies of 14 MeV from deuterium-tritium fusion, and a   
248U fc  few MeV from the fission of {+235}U. The {+248}Cm fraction can be      
248U gc  formed in {+248}U --> {+248}Np --> {+248}Pu -->                        
248U hc  {+248}Am --> {+248}Cm |b{+-} decay chain.                              
248U  c  See also related articles: 1956Fi11, 1967Ho20, 1966Rg01 and 1969In01   
248U  c  Theoretical structure calculations:                                    
248U  c                                                                         
248U  c  2023Zh09: Production cross section calculation using dinuclear system  
248U 2c  model                                                                  
248U  c  2019Qu03: Calculated g.s. binding energy, charge radius, S(2n),        
248U 2c  level density parameter                                                
248U  c  2014Ro09, 2013Gi06: Calculated fission barriers                        
248U  c  2011Ni06: Calculated contributions of the Coulomb, surface symmetry,   
248U 2c  curvature, and surface terms of fission isomers                        
248U  c  2010To07: Calculated proton and neutron single-particle spectra,       
248U 2c  S(n), rms charge radius                                                
248U   L 0            0+                                                       ?
248U 2 L %B-=?                                                                  
248U  cL $Through the detection of {+248}Cm (T{-1/2}=3.48|*10{+5} y) in the     
248U 2cL debris of the thermonuclear test, abundance of {+248}U was deduced to  
248U 3cL be 1.2|*10{+-6} {I1} relative to 1.000 {I4} for {+239}U (1960Di03)     
248U  cL $|b{+-} is expected to be the dominant decay mode of {+248}U, since    
248U 2cL the theoretical half-life for |a decay is >10{+20} s (2019Mo01)        
248U  cL T$|b decay half-life=13.1 s (theory,2019Mo01)                          

